"# Technical & UI/UX Design System Specification: Cursor Modular Rulebook (.mdc)

> **StitchMCP Compatible**: This document specifies authoritative design tokens, component patterns, and negative guardrails for Next.js / React / TypeScript.

## 1. Visual Language & Semantic Tokens
- **Canvas / Background**: Pitch-black or deep dark neutral (\`#09090B\` / \`#121215\`)
- **Card & Surface**: Elevated surface (\`#18181B\`) with \`border-zinc-800/60\`
- **Accent Tokens**: High-contrast indicator for active states and primary CTAs
- **Typography**: Monospace font for code/data metrics, sans-serif for UI labels
- **Styling Paradigm**: Tailwind CSS

## 2. Component Hierarchy & Layout Patterns
- **Single-Canvas Layout**: Avoid nested card-in-card containers. Keep UI depth to a single clean layer.
- **Status & Feedback**: Explicit status indicators (execution latency, item count, validation state).
- **Responsive Adaptability**: Desktop-first layout with graceful degradation for tablet and mobile viewports.

## 3. Core Architectural Conventions
guard-clauses
feature-colocated
result-types
typed-schemas

## 4. Negative Design Guardrails (Forbidden Anti-Patterns)
inspect-first
minimal-diffs
concise-direct
verification-driven
preserve-style
- Never introduce nested card-in-card containers.
- Never apply arbitrary inline CSS styles when design system classes exist.
- Never break layout responsiveness on mobile viewports.
- Never bypass accessibility standards (contrast ratios, focus rings, ARIA labels).

## 5. Domain Design Directives
- Keep responses direct, dense, and code-first with minimal conversational filler.
- Never modify unrequested files or remove unrelated comments.
- Always implement explicit error handling for asynchronous code paths.

## 6. Component Reference Pattern
\`\`\`tsx
// Good: Surgical, strictly typed handler with early exit
export async function getSessionUser(req: Request): Promise<User | null> {
  const token = req.headers.get("authorization")?.replace("Bearer ", "");
  if (!token) return null;
  return verifySessionToken(token);
}
\`\`\`

## 7. Design Verification Gate
Before approving UI modifications:
1. Inspect active file context and target globs before proposing edits.
2. Deliver surgical, focused diffs rather than re-outputting entire files.
3. Enforce strict TypeScript typing: avoid loose 'any' or unsafe type assertions.
4. Adhere to existing repository naming conventions, quote styles, and directory idioms.
5. Provide actionable verification commands and test steps with every modification.
"
`;

